Package: mwperm
Type: Package
Title: Invariant Permutation Tests for Multi-Way Clustered and Panel Regression
Version: 0.4.2
Authors@R: c(
    person("Andrew", "Chen", email = "andrewc24@uchicago.edu", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph")),
    person("Wenxuan", "Guo", email = "wxguo@chicagobooth.edu", role = c("aut", "cph")),
    person("Panos", "Toulis", email = "ptoulis@chicagobooth.edu", role = c("aut", "cph"))
  )
Description: Finite-sample valid hypothesis tests and confidence intervals for
    regression coefficients under multi-way (e.g. dyadic) clustering, including
    panel and missing-data designs. The methods implement the invariant
    permutation test of Guo, W., Toulis, P. and Wang, Y. (2026)
    <doi:10.48550/arXiv.2601.08610>. Validity requires only an invariance of
    the errors conditional on the covariates: separate exchangeability across
    the clustering dimensions for the permutation tests, or double sign
    symmetry of the error array under row-and-column sign changes for the
    heteroskedasticity-robust sign-flip variant (independent symmetric
    errors with any variance pattern; not additive cluster effects). The
    covariate distribution is unrestricted, so the tests remain
    exact in finite samples even with few clusters or heavy-tailed regressors,
    where multi-way cluster-robust standard errors and the wild cluster
    bootstrap can be badly miscalibrated. A block-cyclic permutation group (or
    a sign-flip group) is constructed for the clustering dimensions, nuisance
    covariates are partialled out by the Frisch-Waugh-Lovell projection, and
    confidence intervals are obtained by test inversion. Specialised routines
    cover two-way (dyadic) designs, three-way clustering, panels with arbitrary
    time effects, incomplete and unbalanced panels (keeping the best-covered
    periods when pairs miss some), replicated two-way layouts, irregular
    layouts in which the covariate is constant within cells, incomplete
    arrays via fully observed bicliques,
    and dyadic designs with arbitrary heteroskedasticity and errors
    independent across cells.
